1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,080
Welcome to Lecture Four of AI-Driven Animation and
Video Generation. Today we move from understanding an
image generator

2
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:13,520
to making deliberate choices across an artwork or a
film. We will follow five survey papers.

3
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:18,580
Surveys are useful because they organize many
individual methods into a map of the field.

4
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:22,360
Our purpose is to read that map and connect it to
creative work.

5
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:26,880
Throughout the lecture, ask two questions. What
information does a method need?

6
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:30,860
And what decision does it help an artist make? The
figures come from the papers,

7
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:35,429
while the studio example we develop together is a
classroom exercise.

8
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:40,869
Here is our route. We begin with video models and the
problem of generating a sequence that holds

9
00:00:40,869 --> 00:00:48,109
together through time. We then consider visual art,
where the intended experience determines which controls
matter.

10
00:00:48,109 --> 00:00:53,609
The filmmaking survey shows how creators combine these
tools in actual production workflows.

11
00:00:53,609 --> 00:00:58,229
Visual computing connects images and video to objects,
scenes, and movement data.

12
00:00:58,229 --> 00:01:04,269
Finally, the creative-industries review places
generation within a wider production process. You do
not need to memorize every

13
00:01:04,269 --> 00:01:08,369
model name. By the end, you should be able to choose an
appropriate kind of input,

14
00:01:08,369 --> 00:01:12,139
explain a likely limitation, and describe how you would
judge the result.

15
00:01:12,439 --> 00:01:16,799
What changes when an image starts to move? A still
image suggests possibilities,

16
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:21,999
but it leaves the next moment open. A video commits to
a sequence of events.

17
00:01:21,999 --> 00:01:27,299
If a character raises a hand, that hand needs to belong
to the same person as it moves.

18
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:31,619
If the camera passes a chair, the chair should remain
in a plausible position.

19
00:01:31,619 --> 00:01:37,819
These requirements involve appearance, movement, and
spatial relationships. They also involve meaning.

20
00:01:37,819 --> 00:01:43,319
Holding on an empty room for ten seconds creates a
different experience from showing it for one second.

21
00:01:43,319 --> 00:01:49,574
Time therefore adds both technical demands and artistic
choices. A sequence can fail in either respect.

22
00:01:49,874 --> 00:01:54,254
We will use a simple studio scene throughout the
lecture. The room is empty.

23
00:01:54,254 --> 00:01:59,434
A painting begins to move. Then someone returns. This
is an invented teaching example,

24
00:01:59,434 --> 00:02:04,634
so there is no correct finished version to reproduce.
You might imagine a frightening scene,

25
00:02:04,634 --> 00:02:10,174
a quiet memory, or a playful animation. Start by
picturing what should stay stable.

26
00:02:10,174 --> 00:02:15,574
Perhaps the room layout and painting frame remain fixed
while only the painted surface changes.

27
00:02:15,574 --> 00:02:21,114
Then consider what the returning person should notice.
These decisions will help us compare methods.

28
00:02:21,114 --> 00:02:25,196
A tool becomes useful when we can say which part of
this scene it should control.

29
00:02:25,496 --> 00:02:29,316
Our opening reading is Wang and colleagues' Survey of
Video Diffusion Models,

30
00:02:29,316 --> 00:02:35,336
first posted in 2025 and revised in February 2026. It
covers foundations,

31
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:41,276
implementations, and applications. We will follow its
progression from the generation mechanism to inputs,

32
00:02:41,276 --> 00:02:48,456
control, editing, and connections with three-
dimensional scenes. A survey figure often contains
several methods at once.

33
00:02:48,456 --> 00:02:53,176
Read such a figure as a set of relationships, rather
than as one model you must implement.

34
00:02:53,176 --> 00:02:59,756
Also pay attention to dates. A survey can contain
important earlier examples alongside newer research.

35
00:02:59,756 --> 00:03:05,405
Its publication or revision date does not make every
comparison a current ranking of available systems.

36
00:03:05,705 --> 00:03:10,425
At a high level, video generation connects inputs to a
model and then to a sequence.

37
00:03:10,425 --> 00:03:14,425
The inputs might include a sentence, an image, a source
video, sound,

38
00:03:14,425 --> 00:03:20,325
or camera information. Each input supplies a different
kind of constraint. A sentence can describe a mood or

39
00:03:20,325 --> 00:03:28,205
action, but it usually leaves many visible details
unspecified. An image supplies more exact information
about appearance.

40
00:03:28,205 --> 00:03:32,905
A source video can supply a performance or a movement
pattern. When reading the overview,

41
00:03:32,905 --> 00:03:38,505
look for what enters the model and what comes out. This
simple habit makes a complicated architecture easier

42
00:03:38,505 --> 00:03:43,117
to understand and helps you recognize what a
demonstration actually controls.

43
00:03:43,417 --> 00:03:48,377
Consider three routes to the studio scene. With text-
to-video, we describe the room and the moving painting,

44
00:03:48,377 --> 00:03:53,677
and the model invents much of their appearance. With
image-to-video, we first choose a picture of the room,

45
00:03:53,677 --> 00:03:59,137
then ask for movement from that starting appearance.
With video-to-video, we begin with an existing sequence
and change

46
00:03:59,137 --> 00:04:06,037
selected properties, such as its visual style. None of
these routes automatically solves every production
problem.

47
00:04:06,037 --> 00:04:09,897
An initial image does not guarantee stable identity
later in the shot.

48
00:04:09,897 --> 00:04:16,997
A source video does not guarantee exact motion
preservation. Choose the route according to the
information you already

49
00:04:16,997 --> 00:04:19,678
have and the properties you need to retain.

50
00:04:19,978 --> 00:04:25,898
Diffusion is easiest to understand by separating
training from generation. During training,

51
00:04:25,898 --> 00:04:32,918
a system encounters examples with added noise and
learns how to predict information needed to remove that
noise.

52
00:04:32,918 --> 00:04:38,958
During generation, it starts with a noisy sample and
repeatedly refines it using the learned model.

53
00:04:38,958 --> 00:04:43,798
The diagram shows a succession of states, rather than a
film of a face changing over time.

54
00:04:43,798 --> 00:04:48,738
The prompt or another condition influences the
refinement. The model does not learn a new set of
weights

55
00:04:48,738 --> 00:04:53,578
every time you generate an image. It uses what training
has already established.

56
00:04:53,578 --> 00:04:57,839
This distinction helps explain why changing a prompt
differs from adapting the model.

57
00:04:58,139 --> 00:05:04,439
Video diffusion has two different meanings of time.
Frame time is the time inside the represented event:

58
00:05:04,439 --> 00:05:08,039
a person enters, walks across the studio, and looks at
the painting.

59
00:05:08,039 --> 00:05:13,739
Denoising time is the sequence of computational
refinement steps that produces the sample.

60
00:05:13,739 --> 00:05:17,219
A denoising step is not an extra frame in the finished
video.

61
00:05:17,219 --> 00:05:22,059
Depending on the architecture, one step may update
information for many frames together.

62
00:05:22,059 --> 00:05:27,519
Confusing these two timelines makes diagrams difficult
to read. Whenever you see a time index,

63
00:05:27,519 --> 00:05:32,279
ask whether it identifies a moment in the scene or a
stage in noise removal.

64
00:05:32,279 --> 00:05:35,935
The answer tells you what relationship the model is
trying to represent.

65
00:05:36,235 --> 00:05:41,735
The generation pipeline often works in a compressed
space. First, a text encoder converts the prompt into a

66
00:05:41,735 --> 00:05:48,275
representation that the model can use. The generative
network then refines a noisy representation under that
condition.

67
00:05:48,275 --> 00:05:54,415
A decoder converts the result into visible frames. This
division reduces the amount of information processed by
the

68
00:05:54,415 --> 00:06:00,775
most expensive part of the system. It also means that
the final image depends on several components.

69
00:06:00,775 --> 00:06:06,235
A problem with fine texture may involve compression or
decoding as well as generation.

70
00:06:06,235 --> 00:06:13,175
For our studio scene, the prompt supplies an
instruction, while the latent representation carries
the evolving visual content

71
00:06:13,175 --> 00:06:14,459
during refinement.

72
00:06:14,759 --> 00:06:20,379
A latent is a compressed representation of the data.
Imagine describing the essential structure of a room
with

73
00:06:20,379 --> 00:06:24,559
fewer values than you would need to list every colored
pixel in every frame.

74
00:06:24,559 --> 00:06:29,519
The encoder maps the original material into that
representation, and the decoder maps it back.

75
00:06:29,519 --> 00:06:33,959
The figure shows compression across space and, in some
stages, across time.

76
00:06:33,959 --> 00:06:39,019
Compression makes a large video more manageable, but it
can also discard detail.

77
00:06:39,019 --> 00:06:43,099
A latent is not a miniature image that we can always
interpret directly.

78
00:06:43,099 --> 00:06:49,499
It is a learned representation. Keep the practical
tradeoff in mind: less computation can come with limits
on

79
00:06:49,499 --> 00:06:52,235
small details and reconstruction fidelity.

80
00:06:52,535 --> 00:06:57,675
Spatial relationships occur within a frame. They
include the position of a person relative to a door,

81
00:06:57,675 --> 00:07:03,915
or the relationship between a hand and the object it
holds. Temporal relationships connect different frames.

82
00:07:03,915 --> 00:07:07,295
They help a model relate the hand now to the hand a
moment later.

83
00:07:07,295 --> 00:07:11,915
The diagram separates spatial and temporal processing
to make those roles visible.

84
00:07:11,915 --> 00:07:17,815
Attention is one way to connect relevant features, but
it does not guarantee correct understanding of the
scene.

85
00:07:17,815 --> 00:07:24,035
For the studio example, spatial processing helps
organize the room at a particular instant.

86
00:07:24,035 --> 00:07:30,140
Temporal processing helps the painting and furniture
maintain meaningful relationships as the sequence
develops.

87
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:35,040
This figure illustrates another way to connect frames:
encode information within each frame,

88
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:41,800
then connect those representations across the sequence.
The pictured architecture is a video-understanding
example with a classification output,

89
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:47,080
rather than a complete video generator. We are using it
to understand an architectural idea.

90
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:53,280
The lower part handles appearance within individual
frames. The upper part combines information through
time.

91
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:59,120
Separating these roles can make processing easier to
organize. When you encounter a research diagram,

92
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:05,640
check the output label before assuming its task.
Similar building blocks can appear in recognition and
generation systems,

93
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:09,794
even though their training objectives and outputs
differ substantially.

94
00:08:10,094 --> 00:08:15,634
Some approaches build a sequence in parts, using
earlier outputs to guide what comes next.

95
00:08:15,634 --> 00:08:21,614
This is the basic idea of autoregressive prediction.
The diagram includes script information,

96
00:08:21,614 --> 00:08:27,834
keyframes, and a rendering process. You can think of
the earlier material as context for the next decision.

97
00:08:27,834 --> 00:08:32,074
This can help extend a sequence, but errors in the
context may also carry forward.

98
00:08:32,074 --> 00:08:36,954
If a character's coat changes in one generated part,
later parts may preserve the new coat instead of

99
00:08:36,954 --> 00:08:44,274
the original one. For filmmaking, that makes
checkpoints valuable. Inspect important keyframes and
transitions before treating a longer

100
00:08:44,274 --> 00:08:46,440
generated sequence as a finished shot.

101
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:51,580
Training data shapes the kinds of results a model can
produce. The figure shows a pipeline that selects

102
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:56,920
clips, filters them, and attaches descriptions. Each
stage changes what reaches training.

103
00:08:56,920 --> 00:09:00,400
A caption can describe an object, an action, or a
camera movement.

104
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:05,960
If the caption misses an important event, the model
receives a weaker connection between language and that
event.

105
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:13,100
Filtering can remove unusable clips, but it also
reflects decisions about what counts as desirable
material.

106
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:19,100
An artist should therefore avoid treating model
behavior as a neutral view of all possible images.

107
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:23,470
It reflects the material, labels, and objectives used
to train the system.

108
00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:30,190
The branching diagram makes filtering visible. A broad
pool of material becomes smaller groups suited to
particular training

109
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:37,290
stages. Some examples may have adequate resolution but
little movement. Others may have useful movement but
poor captions

110
00:09:37,290 --> 00:09:43,110
or visual quality. The important point is that
filtering trades coverage against usable examples.

111
00:09:43,110 --> 00:09:49,090
Removing difficult material may improve average
training quality while leaving gaps in certain actions
or styles.

112
00:09:49,090 --> 00:09:54,630
This matters when your project depends on an unusual
visual language. If a model struggles with that
language,

113
00:09:54,630 --> 00:10:01,090
repeatedly rewriting the prompt may not address the
underlying limitation. The required examples or
relationships may be poorly

114
00:10:01,090 --> 00:10:02,591
represented in training.

115
00:10:02,891 --> 00:10:08,701
A single foundation can support several tasks. The
figure moves from image training toward video training
and then

116
00:10:08,701 --> 00:10:16,331
toward specialized applications such as personalization
or editing. Shared foundations let researchers reuse
learned visual knowledge instead of

117
00:10:16,331 --> 00:10:21,871
starting from nothing for every task. However, task
support still depends on the model and the way it

118
00:10:21,871 --> 00:10:28,191
is adapted. Generating a new character and preserving a
particular existing character are different
requirements.

119
00:10:28,191 --> 00:10:32,031
For our studio scene, a general model might create an
attractive room,

120
00:10:32,031 --> 00:10:36,911
while a specialized workflow might better preserve the
same painting across shots.

121
00:10:36,911 --> 00:10:40,901
Shared origins do not make those capabilities
interchangeable in practice.

122
00:10:41,201 --> 00:10:48,241
This diagram from the Cosmos discussion shows a
pretrained foundation model branching into domain-
specific adaptations.

123
00:10:48,241 --> 00:10:54,361
The custom datasets concern physical settings such as
vehicles and robots. Follow the diagram from the common
base

124
00:10:54,361 --> 00:11:01,241
toward the separate applications. Further training
helps specialize the model to the material and tasks of
a domain.

125
00:11:01,241 --> 00:11:06,241
Adapting a model to an artistic style is a related
idea, but this particular figure does not report

126
00:11:06,241 --> 00:11:11,381
an art experiment. The general lesson is that
specialization requires suitable evidence.

127
00:11:11,381 --> 00:11:18,241
A small, carefully chosen dataset may teach a useful
pattern, while an inconsistent collection may introduce
unwanted associations

128
00:11:18,241 --> 00:11:21,173
or reduce the usefulness of the adaptation.

129
00:11:21,473 --> 00:11:26,453
A reference image can communicate several kinds of
information. It may suggest broad meaning,

130
00:11:26,453 --> 00:11:31,533
such as a dog in a garden. It may specify appearance,
such as the exact markings of one

131
00:11:31,533 --> 00:11:36,533
dog. Or it may constrain structure, such as the
location and outline of the subject.

132
00:11:36,533 --> 00:11:44,513
Different conditioning methods preserve these
properties to different degrees. The figure organizes
several ways to introduce image information.

133
00:11:44,513 --> 00:11:50,473
You do not need to memorize every connection. Instead,
ask what you want the reference to contribute.

134
00:11:50,473 --> 00:11:55,813
For the studio scene, a mood image and a precise room-
layout reference serve different purposes,

135
00:11:55,813 --> 00:11:58,417
even if both enter the workflow as pictures.

136
00:11:58,717 --> 00:12:04,957
Object motion and camera motion are distinct. A person
can cross the studio while the camera remains still.

137
00:12:04,957 --> 00:12:08,337
The camera can also move around a person who does not
move.

138
00:12:08,337 --> 00:12:15,457
In the resulting images, both situations produce
changing pixel positions. A system therefore needs
useful constraints if we

139
00:12:15,457 --> 00:12:20,577
want to control them separately. The figure combines
scene descriptions with camera information.

140
00:12:20,577 --> 00:12:25,697
When planning a shot, specify the intended camera
behavior independently from the action.

141
00:12:25,697 --> 00:12:31,437
Then inspect whether the output respects both. A
visually energetic result may still fail if the camera
moves

142
00:12:31,437 --> 00:12:36,812
when the scene requires a stable viewpoint, or if the
subject freezes during the intended action.

143
00:12:37,112 --> 00:12:42,372
Optical flow describes how locations in one image
correspond to locations in another.

144
00:12:42,372 --> 00:12:48,192
The colored paths in the figure represent a movement
field. Such information can help transfer movement or
guide

145
00:12:48,192 --> 00:12:53,392
the evolution of a generated sequence. Optical flow
describes apparent image movement,

146
00:12:53,392 --> 00:12:58,932
so it can reflect both object motion and camera motion.
It is not a complete reconstruction of the

147
00:12:58,932 --> 00:13:06,692
physical world. Occlusion creates additional difficulty
because something visible in one frame may disappear
behind another object.

148
00:13:06,692 --> 00:13:12,672
For creative work, a movement field can offer more
specific guidance than a sentence such as move
naturally.

149
00:13:12,672 --> 00:13:15,805
Its reliability still depends on the source and the
scene.

150
00:13:16,105 --> 00:13:22,305
Sound can guide a visual performance. In this example,
a source image and audio help determine a speaking

151
00:13:22,305 --> 00:13:29,885
face, with additional information related to
expression. Audio supplies timing that a still image
cannot provide.

152
00:13:29,885 --> 00:13:35,985
A voice has pauses, stressed syllables, and changing
rhythm. Mouth movement should relate to those events if
the

153
00:13:35,985 --> 00:13:42,305
result is meant to depict speaking. A convincing
portrait alone does not establish accurate lip
synchronization or a

154
00:13:42,305 --> 00:13:47,945
coherent performance. Watch transitions and listen at
the same time. In a film workflow,

155
00:13:47,945 --> 00:13:52,877
this is also a reminder to plan sound early when it
controls the timing of the visible action.

156
00:13:53,177 --> 00:13:58,497
Editing a video region adds a temporal requirement to a
familiar image-editing task.

157
00:13:58,497 --> 00:14:03,137
If we change the color of a car, that change should
remain attached to the car across the

158
00:14:03,137 --> 00:14:11,377
sequence. The background should also behave
consistently. The examples show local changes and
reconstruction of selected areas.

159
00:14:11,377 --> 00:14:14,477
A mask identifies a region, but the region may move,
change shape,

160
00:14:14,477 --> 00:14:19,037
or become partly hidden. This makes a single successful
frame insufficient evidence.

161
00:14:19,037 --> 00:14:25,137
For the studio exercise, imagine changing only the
painting while preserving the wall and frame.

162
00:14:25,137 --> 00:14:30,846
Inspect the boundary as the camera moves, because that
is where small inconsistencies often become noticeable.

163
00:14:31,146 --> 00:14:37,346
Super-resolution methods can make details look sharper.
These examples compare restoration methods on still
images,

164
00:14:37,346 --> 00:14:41,346
even though the figure appears in a video survey. Read
the columns as methods,

165
00:14:41,346 --> 00:14:46,146
not successive moments in time. Compare the brick
texture and the facial details.

166
00:14:46,146 --> 00:14:50,946
A generated texture can be plausible without matching
the original high-resolution scene.

167
00:14:50,946 --> 00:14:56,246
That difference matters if your aim is faithful
restoration. It may matter differently if you are
designing a

168
00:14:56,246 --> 00:15:02,726
new artwork. For video, an additional question remains:
do the added details stay stable across frames?

169
00:15:02,726 --> 00:15:09,625
These still comparisons alone cannot answer that
question. Sharpness and temporal consistency require
separate inspection.

170
00:15:09,925 --> 00:15:16,165
Prediction and interpolation answer different
questions. Prediction extends a sequence beyond what we
already observe.

171
00:15:16,165 --> 00:15:21,445
Interpolation fills a gap between known moments. The
examples also include generating earlier content,

172
00:15:21,445 --> 00:15:26,645
which asks what might have happened before the
available sequence. These outputs are plausible
constructions,

173
00:15:26,645 --> 00:15:30,925
rather than recovered facts about an unseen event. For
our studio film,

174
00:15:30,925 --> 00:15:37,885
interpolation might help connect two chosen keyframes,
while prediction might extend a shot after the painting
starts moving.

175
00:15:37,885 --> 00:15:43,505
Both methods can produce unexpected intermediate
actions. Check the whole path between important
moments,

176
00:15:43,505 --> 00:15:48,661
because getting the beginning and ending right does not
guarantee that the transition serves the scene.

177
00:15:48,961 --> 00:15:54,241
A recognizable subject needs to survive changes in
pose, lighting, and viewpoint.

178
00:15:54,241 --> 00:15:59,981
The figure compares approaches that use subject-
specific information during adaptation or inference.

179
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:04,541
The technical details differ, but the creative
requirement is easy to state.

180
00:16:04,541 --> 00:16:08,401
We want to recognize the same subject when the camera
angle changes.

181
00:16:08,401 --> 00:16:13,921
A general description such as a small brown dog leaves
many identifying features unspecified.

182
00:16:13,921 --> 00:16:20,681
References can provide those details more directly. For
a film, evaluate identity across the shots you actually
need,

183
00:16:20,681 --> 00:16:25,521
rather than only in a favorable close-up. A method may
preserve a face from the front while struggling

184
00:16:25,521 --> 00:16:28,101
with a profile or an unusual expression.

185
00:16:28,401 --> 00:16:34,861
A good first frame is only the beginning of continuity.
Look at appearance and movement throughout the shot.

186
00:16:34,861 --> 00:16:41,861
Does the subject keep its recognizable features? Does
its motion develop smoothly and plausibly for the
intended style?

187
00:16:41,861 --> 00:16:48,601
The figure presents examples intended to illustrate
more consistent conditioning. Treat them as selected
demonstrations,

188
00:16:48,601 --> 00:16:55,021
not proof that every output will remain stable. Also
separate a deliberate transformation from an accidental
drift.

189
00:16:55,021 --> 00:17:00,041
If our painting is meant to change, specify what may
change and what must remain stable.

190
00:17:00,041 --> 00:17:05,174
The picture inside the frame might transform while the
frame itself keeps the same shape and position.

191
00:17:05,474 --> 00:17:12,854
Video models can incorporate explicit three-dimensional
information. The figure groups examples involving
training datasets,

192
00:17:12,854 --> 00:17:20,074
camera representations, and architectural changes. The
middle column connects a camera to rays through image
locations.

193
00:17:20,074 --> 00:17:24,394
You can understand a ray as a direction from which the
camera observes the scene.

194
00:17:24,394 --> 00:17:30,414
This provides more specific spatial information than a
verbal instruction alone. The right column shows ways
models incorporate

195
00:17:30,414 --> 00:17:36,874
such information. We are interested in the principle:
viewpoint information can help connect images of the
same scene.

196
00:17:36,874 --> 00:17:43,474
It still does not guarantee perfect geometry. Unseen
surfaces and difficult camera paths remain important
places to look

197
00:17:43,474 --> 00:17:44,380
for mistakes.

198
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:49,340
A video model can also provide knowledge for
constructing a scene representation.

199
00:17:49,340 --> 00:17:54,100
The left side of the diagram identifies sources of
visual or motion information.

200
00:17:54,100 --> 00:17:59,860
The middle describes ways to transfer that information.
The right shows representations such as meshes,

201
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:04,660
neural fields, and Gaussian splats. A video is a set of
images over time.

202
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:10,760
A scene representation aims to support rendering under
chosen viewpoints, and sometimes at chosen moments.

203
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:17,400
Moving from one to the other requires reconstruction or
optimization. This matters to artists because a
reusable scene

204
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:23,140
can support later camera decisions, while a finished
video usually commits you to the views already present
in

205
00:18:23,140 --> 00:18:23,949
its frames.

206
00:18:24,249 --> 00:18:32,069
Wonderland illustrates a connection between video
representations and scene reconstruction. Follow the
diagram from the latent space on

207
00:18:32,069 --> 00:18:37,549
the left to the three-dimensional outputs on the right.
The small paired views help show the purpose of

208
00:18:37,549 --> 00:18:43,149
reconstruction: we can render related views of a scene
rather than simply display one image.

209
00:18:43,149 --> 00:18:48,649
The examples use Gaussian splats as a scene
representation. We will revisit that idea later.

210
00:18:48,649 --> 00:18:53,769
For now, ask what additional freedom this creates for a
filmmaker. You may gain some camera flexibility,

211
00:18:53,769 --> 00:18:59,429
but selected views do not demonstrate unrestricted
movement or complete hidden geometry.

212
00:18:59,429 --> 00:19:02,408
Test the camera path your project actually requires.

213
00:19:02,708 --> 00:19:09,288
A dynamic scene changes in two ways: the viewpoint can
change, and the action can advance through time.

214
00:19:09,288 --> 00:19:18,088
CAT4D addresses this combination. The examples include
captured and generated source material connected to
dynamic scene reconstruction.

215
00:19:18,088 --> 00:19:23,728
Imagine freezing a dancer at one moment and walking
around them. Now imagine keeping the camera still while

216
00:19:23,728 --> 00:19:30,108
the dancer continues. These are different operations. A
model that represents both needs to infer appearances
across viewpoint

217
00:19:30,108 --> 00:19:34,208
and time. Much of that material may never have been
observed directly.

218
00:19:34,208 --> 00:19:39,568
For creative work, this offers flexibility, but it also
means that the quality of inferred regions and motions

219
00:19:39,568 --> 00:19:41,571
needs deliberate evaluation.

220
00:19:41,871 --> 00:19:47,271
Let us pause and apply the video survey to our studio.
What fixes the appearance of the room?

221
00:19:47,271 --> 00:19:51,845
An initial image or a scene representation might help.
What controls the movement?

222
00:19:51,845 --> 00:19:58,271
A motion reference, audio signal, camera instruction,
or other condition might supply useful information.

223
00:19:58,271 --> 00:20:04,271
What still needs inspection? The painting boundary, the
person's identity, and the continuity of the room are
possible

224
00:20:04,271 --> 00:20:11,211
answers. Choose one requirement and explain why a
particular input helps. Then identify a failure that
the input

225
00:20:11,211 --> 00:20:15,831
cannot rule out. This exercise is more useful than
naming a fashionable model,

226
00:20:15,831 --> 00:20:19,369
because it connects the method to evidence you can
actually inspect.

227
00:20:19,669 --> 00:20:24,309
Our next reading is Diffusion-Based Visual Art Creation
by Wang, Chen, and Wang,

228
00:20:24,309 --> 00:20:32,589
published in ACM Computing Surveys in 2025. This paper
shifts the emphasis from generating sequences to
understanding artistic

229
00:20:32,589 --> 00:20:38,529
tasks and intentions. We will consider how technical
choices relate to the kind of artwork someone wants to

230
00:20:38,529 --> 00:20:43,769
make. A system may generate a polished image while
giving the artist too little control over its meaning

231
00:20:43,769 --> 00:20:49,169
or structure. Conversely, an imperfect result may
become useful material for further work.

232
00:20:49,169 --> 00:20:54,049
Keep the studio example in mind. We now ask what the
changing painting should express,

233
00:20:54,049 --> 00:20:57,445
and how that intention changes the way we judge its
output.

234
00:20:57,745 --> 00:21:02,885
The figure places diffusion-based visual art at the
intersection of several categories.

235
00:21:02,885 --> 00:21:08,865
Art can use different media, serve different purposes,
and exist as static or changing material.

236
00:21:08,865 --> 00:21:13,245
Technical methods also vary in what they represent and
how they generate it.

237
00:21:13,245 --> 00:21:19,065
The survey studies where these perspectives meet. This
is useful because a technical task label rarely
describes the

238
00:21:19,065 --> 00:21:24,685
whole artistic problem. Text-to-image tells us
something about inputs and outputs, but little about
why the image matters.

239
00:21:24,685 --> 00:21:30,945
For our studio, an illustration, an installation, and a
film might use related generated imagery while
requiring different

240
00:21:30,945 --> 00:21:34,944
forms of control and different ways of evaluating the
audience experience.

241
00:21:35,244 --> 00:21:42,064
Text becomes usable conditioning through an encoder.
The diagram shows a conceptual connection between text
representations,

242
00:21:42,064 --> 00:21:49,884
image representations, and a denoising network. CLIP is
associated with learning relationships between image
and text representations.

243
00:21:49,884 --> 00:21:55,144
In a diffusion workflow, text information can then
influence the refinement of visual content.

244
00:21:55,144 --> 00:22:01,024
This simplified figure combines ideas and should not be
treated as the exact architecture of every generator.

245
00:22:01,024 --> 00:22:06,164
The important point is that a sentence becomes a
numerical representation, rather than a complete set of
drawing

246
00:22:06,164 --> 00:22:11,864
instructions. A phrase such as uneasy memory therefore
leaves many visible decisions open.

247
00:22:11,864 --> 00:22:15,259
References and editing can help make those decisions
more specific.

248
00:22:15,559 --> 00:22:22,739
The survey identifies three research perspectives:
applications, generation methods, and understanding or
data.

249
00:22:22,739 --> 00:22:27,199
The overlapping circles show that a paper may belong to
more than one perspective.

250
00:22:27,199 --> 00:22:33,959
Work on an artistic application can also introduce a
generation method or study how people interpret images.

251
00:22:33,959 --> 00:22:39,019
The numbers describe the authors' selected research
corpus, not all possible work in the field.

252
00:22:39,019 --> 00:22:43,999
When reading a paper, identify which question it
primarily answers. Does it introduce a tool,

253
00:22:43,999 --> 00:22:50,019
study an audience, or analyze material? This helps you
avoid expecting a technical benchmark to answer an
artistic

254
00:22:50,019 --> 00:22:53,399
question that the researchers did not set out to
investigate.

255
00:22:53,699 --> 00:22:57,979
An artistic intention becomes useful when we connect it
to visible decisions.

256
00:22:57,979 --> 00:23:04,639
The survey's framework links scenario, modality, task,
and method to artistic requirements and evaluation.

257
00:23:04,639 --> 00:23:08,739
Suppose our intention is an uneasy memory. We could use
delayed movement,

258
00:23:08,739 --> 00:23:15,419
missing details, or a familiar room with one altered
object. Each choice suggests different controls.

259
00:23:15,419 --> 00:23:20,159
A general request to make the scene more artistic does
not tell us what to change or how

260
00:23:20,159 --> 00:23:25,159
to judge success. Start with the intended experience,
identify a visible decision,

261
00:23:25,159 --> 00:23:29,919
and choose a method that gives you some control over
it. The framework is a way to organize

262
00:23:29,919 --> 00:23:31,133
that reasoning.

263
00:23:31,433 --> 00:23:35,573
A scenario gives the work a purpose. The enlarged
figure distinguishes medium,

264
00:23:35,573 --> 00:23:42,213
genre, and style. A portrait concentrates attention
differently from a landscape. A film unfolds over time,

265
00:23:42,213 --> 00:23:47,153
while an installation may respond to where a visitor
stands or how long they remain.

266
00:23:47,153 --> 00:23:53,333
These distinctions affect both production and
evaluation. The same generated image might be a
finished print,

267
00:23:53,333 --> 00:23:58,373
a storyboard reference, or one frame in an animation.
For the studio example,

268
00:23:58,373 --> 00:24:04,293
decide whether the audience watches a fixed sequence or
discovers changes while moving through a space.

269
00:24:04,293 --> 00:24:08,035
That choice changes the kinds of continuity and control
the work needs.

270
00:24:08,335 --> 00:24:13,815
A modality is the form of the material you work with.
The figure names a three-dimensional scene,

271
00:24:13,815 --> 00:24:19,395
a two-dimensional image, and a brush stroke. These
forms support different kinds of intervention.

272
00:24:19,395 --> 00:24:24,675
Editing pixels gives direct access to an image surface.
Working with a scene may give access to camera

273
00:24:24,675 --> 00:24:32,215
position and spatial relationships. A stroke
representation may preserve information about how a
mark is constructed.

274
00:24:32,215 --> 00:24:37,355
None is universally best. Ask what you want to revise
later. If you need to move the camera

275
00:24:37,355 --> 00:24:42,075
around the studio, a single finished image may be
restrictive. If you need a fixed composition,

276
00:24:42,075 --> 00:24:44,211
its simplicity may be helpful.

277
00:24:44,511 --> 00:24:49,491
Four useful creative tasks are generating content,
controlling a result, changing style,

278
00:24:49,491 --> 00:24:56,111
and editing selected content. These tasks often occur
together, but distinguishing them helps diagnose a
workflow.

279
00:24:56,111 --> 00:25:00,811
If the room is missing, you need content generation. If
the room exists but the door is in

280
00:25:00,811 --> 00:25:06,511
the wrong place, you need control or editing. If the
composition is suitable but the material should
resemble

281
00:25:06,511 --> 00:25:11,951
charcoal, you may need stylization. Describe the
problem before choosing the tool.

282
00:25:11,951 --> 00:25:18,711
Repeating full generation for every small correction
can discard successful decisions that you would rather
preserve.

283
00:25:18,711 --> 00:25:21,903
A local intervention may be a better fit for the actual
need.

284
00:25:22,203 --> 00:25:27,883
A mask specifies a region where an edit may occur. The
surrounding image provides context for making the

285
00:25:27,883 --> 00:25:32,883
change fit. In our studio, we might mask the inside of
the painting while leaving its frame and

286
00:25:32,883 --> 00:25:38,463
the wall outside the selected region. This makes the
request more precise than asking for a different
painting

287
00:25:38,463 --> 00:25:45,143
in an entirely new room. However, a mask is not a
guarantee that every unselected detail will remain

288
00:25:45,143 --> 00:25:51,063
identical. The behavior depends on the method and
settings. Inspect the edge of the edit,

289
00:25:51,063 --> 00:25:57,802
the lighting, and nearby textures. A successful local
change should also make sense within the larger
composition.

290
00:25:58,102 --> 00:26:04,762
Style includes more than color or surface texture. The
diagram connects local details with global information
and shows

291
00:26:04,762 --> 00:26:09,682
how different parts of a method can influence a result.
Think about the difference between a face painted

292
00:26:09,682 --> 00:26:15,322
with loose brushwork and a face whose proportions have
also changed. Both may appear stylistic,

293
00:26:15,322 --> 00:26:21,502
but they alter different relationships. Composition,
shape, and rhythm can be part of the visual language.

294
00:26:21,502 --> 00:26:26,442
When you request a style transfer, identify which of
those properties may change.

295
00:26:26,442 --> 00:26:32,442
Otherwise, a method might produce the intended texture
while removing a structural feature that was important
to the

296
00:26:32,442 --> 00:26:34,661
identity or meaning of the original.

297
00:26:34,961 --> 00:26:41,321
Let us separate content and style as a practical
exercise. Imagine that a character must remain
recognizable while

298
00:26:41,321 --> 00:26:46,201
the image changes from photographic to painted. Which
features should stay stable?

299
00:26:46,201 --> 00:26:51,221
You might choose the silhouette, facial proportions, or
pose. Which features may vary?

300
00:26:51,221 --> 00:26:57,061
Brushwork and palette are possible answers. This
separation is useful, but it is not universal.

301
00:26:57,061 --> 00:27:03,321
Some styles depend on changing proportions or spatial
relationships. Explain your own boundary rather than
assuming the software

302
00:27:03,321 --> 00:27:09,441
will infer it. For the studio painting, decide whether
its subject remains recognizable during the
transformation,

303
00:27:09,441 --> 00:27:13,505
or whether losing recognition is part of the intended
experience.

304
00:27:13,805 --> 00:27:18,125
A technical score answers a particular question. It may
estimate prompt alignment,

305
00:27:18,125 --> 00:27:24,665
visual similarity, or some aspect of perceptual
quality. It cannot automatically establish whether an
artwork succeeds.

306
00:27:24,665 --> 00:27:31,025
A deliberately ambiguous image might receive a weaker
score for literal prompt matching while producing the
intended experience.

307
00:27:31,025 --> 00:27:36,205
That does not make technical evaluation useless. It
means we need to state what each measure is supposed

308
00:27:36,205 --> 00:27:41,985
to assess. For the studio, you might separately
evaluate whether the room remains stable and whether
the change

309
00:27:41,985 --> 00:27:48,105
in the painting feels unsettling. One concerns a
visible constraint. The other concerns interpretation,

310
00:27:48,105 --> 00:27:52,689
which may require viewers and discussion rather than a
single automated number.

311
00:27:52,989 --> 00:27:59,129
This chart records changes in the survey's selected
literature over time. The labels connect the
distribution to influential

312
00:27:59,129 --> 00:28:05,369
developments such as diffusion models and methods for
adaptation or control. Read it as a historical view of

313
00:28:05,369 --> 00:28:09,729
the corpus the authors assembled. It is not a complete
census of AI-art research,

314
00:28:09,729 --> 00:28:15,729
and its final dates do not extend to today. The useful
question is how new methods changed the

315
00:28:15,729 --> 00:28:22,409
kinds of tasks researchers could attempt. Greater
access to generation can shift attention toward
control,

316
00:28:22,409 --> 00:28:28,089
editing, and applications. To understand the strength
of that claim, we would still need to examine the
actual

317
00:28:28,089 --> 00:28:30,125
papers and selection process.

318
00:28:30,425 --> 00:28:36,805
The word clouds offer another view of the authors'
research collection. Larger words indicate prominence
within the coded

319
00:28:36,805 --> 00:28:44,245
material, rather than artistic importance or current
popularity. Compare task terms such as generation and
editing with method

320
00:28:44,245 --> 00:28:49,385
terms such as diffusion. Then look at the artistic
categories and user requirements.

321
00:28:49,385 --> 00:28:54,105
These are different kinds of vocabulary, and mixing
them can make a project description vague.

322
00:28:54,105 --> 00:28:57,125
If you say your project uses diffusion, you have named
a method.

323
00:28:57,125 --> 00:29:03,845
If you say it explores fragmented memory through
changing portraits, you have begun to describe an
artistic purpose.

324
00:29:03,845 --> 00:29:07,496
A clear proposal explains the connection between those
two descriptions.

325
00:29:07,796 --> 00:29:14,616
Human involvement changes across a workflow. The figure
presents possible roles for people and AI,

326
00:29:14,616 --> 00:29:20,916
from assistance and analysis toward more extensive
generation. Treat this as a conceptual perspective,

327
00:29:20,916 --> 00:29:25,776
not an inevitable sequence in which one role replaces
another. Choosing references,

328
00:29:25,776 --> 00:29:31,076
rejecting outputs, setting constraints, and deciding
when to stop are all meaningful parts of creative work.

329
00:29:31,076 --> 00:29:35,636
Their importance may increase when generating more
candidates becomes easy. For your own project,

330
00:29:35,636 --> 00:29:40,556
identify a decision that you would keep under direct
human control. Then explain what evidence you would
want

331
00:29:40,556 --> 00:29:43,651
from a tool before accepting its contribution to that
decision.

332
00:29:43,951 --> 00:29:48,271
Return to the studio as an artwork. Our intention is an
uneasy memory,

333
00:29:48,271 --> 00:29:52,851
and our chosen intervention is that the painting
changes before the room does.

334
00:29:52,851 --> 00:29:57,851
The order directs attention. At first, the viewer may
wonder whether they noticed anything.

335
00:29:57,851 --> 00:30:03,591
Later, a second change can confirm that something is
wrong. A different version might use abrupt movement
and

336
00:30:03,591 --> 00:30:10,471
loud sound to create surprise instead. Both could use
similar generation tools while producing different
experiences.

337
00:30:10,471 --> 00:30:15,991
Describe the intended effect in terms that can guide
production. We need to know where the change begins,

338
00:30:15,991 --> 00:30:19,892
how quickly it develops, and which stable details help
the audience notice it.

339
00:30:20,192 --> 00:30:26,632
Choose one artistic intention and one visible
intervention. For example, you might want the studio to
feel welcoming,

340
00:30:26,632 --> 00:30:31,952
and decide that light gradually enters through the
window. Or you might want it to feel unfamiliar,

341
00:30:31,952 --> 00:30:38,372
and change the scale of an object between shots.
Explain how an audience could read the intervention.

342
00:30:38,372 --> 00:30:43,732
Then propose a way to judge whether it works, perhaps
by showing two versions to classmates and asking

343
00:30:43,732 --> 00:30:49,152
what changed their interpretation. Pause the recording
if you want time to work through an example.

344
00:30:49,152 --> 00:30:53,472
The aim is to connect a controllable property to an
intended experience,

345
00:30:53,472 --> 00:30:56,879
rather than simply to request a more impressive output.

346
00:30:57,179 --> 00:31:02,239
Once art becomes a sequence, decisions have to survive
across shots and moments.

347
00:31:02,239 --> 00:31:07,999
A visual style may need to remain recognizable. A
character may need to carry identity from a close-up

348
00:31:07,999 --> 00:31:13,479
into a wider view. The sequence also creates
relationships through ordering, duration,

349
00:31:13,479 --> 00:31:19,919
and sound. Some art videos deliberately disrupt
continuity, so consistency is not an absolute artistic
rule.

350
00:31:19,919 --> 00:31:24,499
What matters is whether a change supports the work. We
now move from the visual-art framework to filmmaking

351
00:31:24,499 --> 00:31:29,859
cases. As you look at each case, identify the intended
effect, the tools that support it,

352
00:31:29,859 --> 00:31:33,589
and the human choices that connect the outputs into a
finished experience.

353
00:31:33,889 --> 00:31:40,429
The filmmaking survey by Zhang and colleagues appeared
in the 2025 CVPR workshop on Computer Vision for the

354
00:31:40,429 --> 00:31:47,329
Creative Industries. It combines observations from an
AI film hackathon with case studies and feedback from
artists.

355
00:31:47,329 --> 00:31:51,449
This gives us a useful view of how people combine
methods in practice.

356
00:31:51,449 --> 00:31:55,929
It also sets limits on the evidence. Hackathon
participants are a selected group,

357
00:31:55,929 --> 00:32:01,429
and the tools reflect the period studied. We should not
treat their behavior as a complete picture of

358
00:32:01,429 --> 00:32:06,629
the film industry. Focus on what the cases reveal about
workflow: where generation helps,

359
00:32:06,629 --> 00:32:11,878
where creators intervene, and why a film still needs
decisions beyond individual shots.

360
00:32:12,178 --> 00:32:18,838
This workflow comes from DOG: Dream of Galaxy. Follow
the sequence from an image toward a depth map,

361
00:32:18,838 --> 00:32:25,818
a shallow spatial construction, and visual effects. The
generated image is one contribution to the production
process.

362
00:32:25,818 --> 00:32:31,778
Later stages create movement and shape the final
presentation. This is a useful correction to the idea
that

363
00:32:31,778 --> 00:32:35,598
making an AI film means asking one model for a finished
movie.

364
00:32:35,598 --> 00:32:39,418
A creator may combine generation with familiar tools
for composition, camera work,

365
00:32:39,418 --> 00:32:44,118
and editing. When evaluating a workflow, identify the
contribution of each stage.

366
00:32:44,118 --> 00:32:49,036
Otherwise, you may attribute an effect to the generator
that actually comes from later production work.

367
00:32:49,336 --> 00:32:54,616
Depth helps distinguish near and far regions in an
image. If those regions are lifted into a shallow

368
00:32:54,616 --> 00:33:00,816
spatial arrangement, moving a virtual camera can create
different amounts of apparent motion across the
picture.

369
00:33:00,816 --> 00:33:04,676
This is one reason depth can make a still image feel
more spatial.

370
00:33:04,676 --> 00:33:08,936
However, the original image does not show what is
behind each object.

371
00:33:08,936 --> 00:33:16,916
A large camera movement may expose gaps or stretched
regions. The figure's two-and-a-half-dimensional
construction is therefore useful within

372
00:33:16,916 --> 00:33:21,436
a range of views. For the studio scene, a small camera
drift might work well,

373
00:33:21,436 --> 00:33:25,362
while a complete orbit around furniture would demand
much more information.

374
00:33:25,662 --> 00:33:33,322
The survey's Film Hack samples contain eight films from
2023, sixty-seven from 2024,

375
00:33:33,322 --> 00:33:40,022
and one hundred eighteen from 2025. These numbers
provide context for the observations that follow.

376
00:33:40,022 --> 00:33:45,582
The sample size changed, and the participants chose an
event devoted to AI filmmaking.

377
00:33:45,582 --> 00:33:51,562
Adoption rates in this group therefore cannot directly
estimate adoption across the whole industry.

378
00:33:51,562 --> 00:33:57,102
The earliest group is especially small. A useful
reading habit is to ask who supplied the evidence and

379
00:33:57,102 --> 00:34:01,822
how they were selected. This does not dismiss the
survey. It helps us use it appropriately,

380
00:34:01,822 --> 00:34:08,585
as a detailed account of a particular creative setting
rather than a universal industry census.

381
00:34:08,885 --> 00:34:14,505
The 2025 sample used an average of 3.14 video-
generation tools per film.

382
00:34:14,505 --> 00:34:18,485
This describes the sample, rather than recommending
that every film use three tools.

383
00:34:18,485 --> 00:34:24,565
Creators may combine tools because different stages
have different requirements. One method may produce a
useful starting look,

384
00:34:24,565 --> 00:34:29,525
another may preserve a performance, and an editing
application may assemble the results.

385
00:34:29,525 --> 00:34:35,425
Additional tools also introduce coordination work and
opportunities for mismatch. For your project,

386
00:34:35,425 --> 00:34:40,865
add a tool when you can identify the problem it solves.
A workflow with fewer tools can be

387
00:34:40,865 --> 00:34:46,148
effective if it provides the controls you need and lets
you preserve decisions that already work.

388
00:34:46,448 --> 00:34:53,668
The chart compares importance with perceived
performance for four selected tasks. The green bars
represent how important artists

389
00:34:53,668 --> 00:35:00,068
considered the task. The gold bars represent their
assessment of current performance during the survey
period.

390
00:35:00,068 --> 00:35:05,468
Character identity, body movement, camera control, and
local editing all show a gap.

391
00:35:05,468 --> 00:35:12,088
These are ratings from one hundred respondents on a
zero-to-seven scale, not objective model benchmark
scores.

392
00:35:12,088 --> 00:35:18,248
The result suggests that attractive generation alone
did not satisfy all production needs.

393
00:35:18,248 --> 00:35:22,868
Notice how the tasks concern control over specific
properties. For an artist,

394
00:35:22,868 --> 00:35:29,200
being able to repeat or revise a decision can matter as
much as obtaining an impressive first output.

395
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:34,880
A Dream About to Awaken illustrates a workflow in which
drawings help establish the visual language.

396
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:41,180
The survey describes interpreting hand-drawn
storyboards and remixing color and style. Look at the
strong shapes and the

397
00:35:41,180 --> 00:35:46,620
repeated use of bright color in the examples. The
drawings give the process a more specific starting
point

398
00:35:46,620 --> 00:35:52,820
than a broad style label alone. They also preserve a
place for the creator's composition decisions.

399
00:35:52,820 --> 00:35:58,400
When using a generator, you can begin with material you
made yourself and ask it to develop selected

400
00:35:58,400 --> 00:36:06,038
aspects. The important question is which qualities of
that starting material should survive through
interpretation and animation.

401
00:36:06,338 --> 00:36:13,078
Overthinking uses a deliberately nostalgic visual
language. The workflow diagram includes image
adaptation,

402
00:36:13,078 --> 00:36:18,418
asset creation, animation, and editing. Read the
branches as parts of this particular production,

403
00:36:18,418 --> 00:36:23,038
rather than a mandatory recipe. Several techniques
contribute to the final style,

404
00:36:23,038 --> 00:36:29,658
and their contributions occur at different stages. This
matters because viewers experience the finished
sequence as a whole.

405
00:36:29,658 --> 00:36:34,058
They do not separate an image model's contribution from
a timing decision in the edit.

406
00:36:34,058 --> 00:36:39,498
For our own work, we can still separate those
contributions analytically. Doing so helps us decide
where to

407
00:36:39,498 --> 00:36:44,497
intervene when the final sequence feels wrong, even
though the individual images look suitable.

408
00:36:44,797 --> 00:36:50,817
The upper branch of Overthinking begins with a curated
image collection and adaptation of an image model.

409
00:36:50,817 --> 00:36:57,497
The paper discusses imagery associated with mid-century
toys. A LoRA is a compact set of learned changes that

410
00:36:57,497 --> 00:37:04,197
can adapt a larger model to particular material. The
later stages build and refine assets for the scene.

411
00:37:04,197 --> 00:37:10,937
The important creative decision happens before
movement: the team chooses a visual world and prepares
material that supports

412
00:37:10,937 --> 00:37:16,837
it. If you want a coherent art video, collecting
references and deciding what belongs in that world can

413
00:37:16,837 --> 00:37:21,847
be more productive than generating unrelated shots and
trying to make them match afterward.

414
00:37:22,147 --> 00:37:28,887
The lower branches of the workflow show animation and
editing. The survey describes adjusting playback
cadence to evoke

415
00:37:28,887 --> 00:37:34,087
a stop-motion quality. This is a reminder that style
has a temporal dimension.

416
00:37:34,087 --> 00:37:40,587
Two sequences can use similar images and still feel
different because of how movement is sampled or paced.

417
00:37:40,587 --> 00:37:44,827
Smooth motion is appropriate for some intentions, while
a stepped rhythm may suit another.

418
00:37:44,827 --> 00:37:51,187
For the studio painting, ask whether its movement
should flow continuously or arrive in small unsettling
changes.

419
00:37:51,187 --> 00:37:56,587
That choice may involve editing as well as generation.
It should be judged against the intended experience,

420
00:37:56,587 --> 00:37:59,603
rather than against smoothness as a universal goal.

421
00:37:59,903 --> 00:38:07,683
Clown provides a different relationship to consistency.
The survey discusses frame-by-frame style
transformation and interprets visual variation as

422
00:38:07,683 --> 00:38:13,843
supporting a fragmented identity. Look at how the
character's appearance changes while the performance
remains related across the

423
00:38:13,843 --> 00:38:20,683
sequence. An inconsistency can become expressive
material, but that does not make every accidental
change meaningful.

424
00:38:20,683 --> 00:38:25,503
We need an account of how it affects the viewer. This
case is useful because it challenges a

425
00:38:25,503 --> 00:38:33,303
simple rule that all AI artifacts must be removed.
Instead, ask whether a particular variation supports
the work,

426
00:38:33,303 --> 00:38:37,486
distracts from it, or communicates something the
creator did not intend.

427
00:38:37,786 --> 00:38:42,666
Here we enlarge part of the comparison so that the hand
gesture is easier to follow.

428
00:38:42,666 --> 00:38:49,186
Compare corresponding moments across the two rows.
Which features of the performance remain legible even
when the appearance

429
00:38:49,186 --> 00:38:55,506
changes? The position of a hand or the direction of a
gaze can carry an action through stylization.

430
00:38:55,506 --> 00:39:00,086
At the same time, small changes around the face may
alter the character's expression.

431
00:39:00,086 --> 00:39:05,666
These stills help us examine correspondence, but they
cannot establish how smooth the full sequence feels.

432
00:39:05,666 --> 00:39:11,326
For that, we need playback. In your own project,
compare both individual frames and the moving result
before

433
00:39:11,326 --> 00:39:14,281
deciding that a transformation preserves the
performance.

434
00:39:14,581 --> 00:39:18,421
Would a stable face or a changing face better serve
this scene?

435
00:39:18,421 --> 00:39:25,121
There is no answer without an artistic intention. A
conventional dialogue scene may depend on stable
identity so

436
00:39:25,121 --> 00:39:31,641
that the audience follows the conversation. A work
about unstable memory may use changing appearance as
part of

437
00:39:31,641 --> 00:39:36,841
its meaning. Propose one intention for each version,
and explain what the viewer should notice.

438
00:39:36,841 --> 00:39:41,561
Then identify a limit. Even a film about instability
might need a consistent gesture,

439
00:39:41,561 --> 00:39:47,101
setting, or sound to hold the sequence together. Pause
here if you want to discuss alternatives.

440
00:39:47,101 --> 00:39:51,269
The goal is to make continuity a considered choice
rather than an automatic slogan.

441
00:39:51,569 --> 00:39:56,409
For Pixi illustrates a case where a recurring character
needs recognizable features.

442
00:39:56,409 --> 00:40:03,749
Look across the selected images. The blue material,
large eyes, proportions, and general silhouette help
establish continuity,

443
00:40:03,749 --> 00:40:10,089
although the appearances are not identical. The survey
describes iterative generation and region editing as
parts of the

444
00:40:10,089 --> 00:40:16,489
workflow. This is a production process that includes
selecting and correcting, rather than expecting one
instruction to guarantee

445
00:40:16,489 --> 00:40:21,069
every shot. The stills show the kinds of identity cues
the creators work with.

446
00:40:21,069 --> 00:40:26,669
They do not prove perfect consistency across the entire
film. As a viewer or researcher,

447
00:40:26,669 --> 00:40:30,603
keep the difference between a selected example and a
complete evaluation clear.

448
00:40:30,903 --> 00:40:35,023
The enlarged examples let us be more specific about a
character reference.

449
00:40:35,023 --> 00:40:39,723
A phrase such as blue puppet supplies a category, but
it leaves the shape of the eyes,

450
00:40:39,723 --> 00:40:45,463
the head proportions, and the material open. A
reference can communicate these details more directly.

451
00:40:45,463 --> 00:40:52,683
Identify two features that must survive changes in view
and expression. Then decide which variations are
acceptable.

452
00:40:52,683 --> 00:40:57,563
Lighting should be allowed to change, for example,
without changing what the character is made of.

453
00:40:57,563 --> 00:41:03,003
This exercise turns a vague request for consistency
into a set of visible criteria.

454
00:41:03,003 --> 00:41:08,934
Those criteria can guide reference selection, local
editing, and the rejection of unsuitable shots.

455
00:41:09,234 --> 00:41:16,414
Fish Tank connects generated material to a three-
dimensional production workflow. The figure places
finished scenes beside assets and

456
00:41:16,414 --> 00:41:21,394
a scene model. Notice that the final image depends on
how objects are arranged and rendered,

457
00:41:21,394 --> 00:41:28,494
rather than only on their individual appearance. The
case study reports manual work on UV mapping and
topology.

458
00:41:28,494 --> 00:41:34,634
Those are ordinary production requirements that do not
disappear when generation supplies an initial asset.

459
00:41:34,634 --> 00:41:40,074
For a filmmaker, the useful output is something that
can participate in the intended scene.

460
00:41:40,074 --> 00:41:44,734
A beautiful preview may still require substantial work
before the object can be lit,

461
00:41:44,734 --> 00:41:47,607
placed, or animated as the production demands.

462
00:41:47,907 --> 00:41:53,987
Look more closely at the scene construction. Geometry
determines the shape and placement of surfaces.

463
00:41:53,987 --> 00:41:58,647
Topology describes how a mesh connects, which matters
when it needs to deform.

464
00:41:58,647 --> 00:42:05,767
UV mapping associates locations on that surface with
texture information. A generated asset may need
correction in any

465
00:42:05,767 --> 00:42:10,727
of these areas. This is why it helps to decide how an
asset will be used before judging

466
00:42:10,727 --> 00:42:16,827
it. A background object seen briefly from one angle has
different requirements from a character that bends in

467
00:42:16,827 --> 00:42:22,887
a close-up. In the studio, a chair that the returning
person sits on needs more dependable structure than

468
00:42:22,887 --> 00:42:24,637
a distant decorative object.

469
00:42:24,937 --> 00:42:29,737
Volumography refers here to creating moving images
through spatial capture and rendering.

470
00:42:29,737 --> 00:42:35,237
The survey presents examples including Former Garden,
Metanoia, and Dressage: Marching Through Memories.

471
00:42:35,237 --> 00:42:40,897
Instead of recording only a fixed sequence of frames, a
spatial representation can permit later decisions about
the

472
00:42:40,897 --> 00:42:47,617
viewpoint. This opens a different kind of creative
process. Camera movement can become something explored
after capture.

473
00:42:47,617 --> 00:42:53,417
The freedom is still limited by what the representation
contains and how accurately it reconstructs the scene.

474
00:42:53,417 --> 00:42:59,157
Missing surfaces, reflective materials, and
insufficient observations may produce gaps or
distortions.

475
00:42:59,157 --> 00:43:04,440
Such effects can be production problems or, in some
cases, material for artistic interpretation.

476
00:43:04,740 --> 00:43:10,560
This enlarged image shows a fragmentary spatial
appearance. Parts of the scene seem incomplete,

477
00:43:10,560 --> 00:43:16,760
stretched, or loosely connected. In the cases discussed
by the survey, such qualities can contribute to an
experience

478
00:43:16,760 --> 00:43:23,820
of memory or uncertain space. We should avoid assuming
that every visible gap was individually designed.

479
00:43:23,820 --> 00:43:29,300
The artistic decision may instead involve selecting and
framing the behavior of a reconstruction process.

480
00:43:29,300 --> 00:43:32,680
Ask what the fragments direct your attention toward. Do
they suggest loss,

481
00:43:32,680 --> 00:43:39,820
movement, or an unstable viewpoint? Then consider
whether a more complete reconstruction would strengthen
or weaken that reading.

482
00:43:39,820 --> 00:43:43,219
Technical fidelity and expressive effect can point
toward different choices.

483
00:43:43,519 --> 00:43:47,099
A short film still needs decisions about what changes,
when it changes,

484
00:43:47,099 --> 00:43:53,159
and what the audience should feel. Return to the empty
studio. Perhaps the painting moves only after the

485
00:43:53,159 --> 00:43:58,679
viewer has had time to notice its stillness. Perhaps
the returning person never sees what happened.

486
00:43:58,679 --> 00:44:04,899
These choices shape the audience's knowledge and
expectation. A generator can help produce the required
material,

487
00:44:04,899 --> 00:44:10,399
but the relationship between moments still needs
planning. This does not mean every art video must use
conventional

488
00:44:10,399 --> 00:44:17,479
narrative. A work organized through repetition or
rhythm also makes decisions about change and duration.

489
00:44:17,479 --> 00:44:21,273
Describe those decisions clearly enough that you can
evaluate alternatives.

490
00:44:21,573 --> 00:44:25,853
Here is one possible shot plan. The opening establishes
the empty room.

491
00:44:25,853 --> 00:44:30,573
The next shot reveals movement inside the painting. The
ending shows someone returning.

492
00:44:30,573 --> 00:44:34,993
Now make the plan more concrete. Is the first view wide
enough to establish the door and painting

493
00:44:34,993 --> 00:44:40,173
together? Does the reveal use a camera move, an edit,
or movement inside a fixed frame?

494
00:44:40,173 --> 00:44:46,133
Does the returning person's expression need to be
readable? Each answer creates a technical requirement.

495
00:44:46,133 --> 00:44:49,713
You can then decide where to use an initial image, a
motion reference,

496
00:44:49,713 --> 00:44:57,153
or local editing. The shot plan helps connect an
artistic idea to manageable production tasks without
determining every

497
00:44:57,153 --> 00:44:58,645
detail in advance.

498
00:44:58,945 --> 00:45:04,505
The filmmaking cases show several ways to turn
generated material into useful shots and sequences.

499
00:45:04,505 --> 00:45:09,285
Some creators establish style through adapted image
models. Others preserve a source performance,

500
00:45:09,285 --> 00:45:15,585
construct three-dimensional assets, or use spatial
capture. Editing connects these decisions through
timing and order.

501
00:45:15,585 --> 00:45:20,345
A generated shot becomes useful when it serves that
larger sequence. Before moving on,

502
00:45:20,345 --> 00:45:25,685
choose one case that relates to a project you might
make. Name the production problem it addresses and

503
00:45:25,685 --> 00:45:31,405
one limitation you would test. We will now examine
shared technical ideas behind these workflows,

504
00:45:31,405 --> 00:45:38,000
including conditioning, scene representations, and the
difference between generating pixels and generating
movement data.

505
00:45:38,300 --> 00:45:43,420
Our fourth reading is State of the Art on Diffusion
Models for Visual Computing by Po and colleagues,

506
00:45:43,420 --> 00:45:49,580
published in Computer Graphics Forum in 2024. It
connects diffusion methods across images,

507
00:45:49,580 --> 00:45:55,520
video, geometry, and motion. We will use it to explain
why related model ideas can support different kinds

508
00:45:55,520 --> 00:46:01,520
of output. The date matters here: these are
foundational research examples, rather than a claim
about the newest

509
00:46:01,520 --> 00:46:07,260
available products. Focus on the representation and the
control signal. An image,

510
00:46:07,260 --> 00:46:11,260
a scene, and a motion sequence may all use learned
generative knowledge,

511
00:46:11,260 --> 00:46:14,795
but they create different opportunities for later
editing and rendering.

512
00:46:15,095 --> 00:46:19,895
A learned prior captures regularities in the material a
model has seen during training.

513
00:46:19,895 --> 00:46:24,895
In practical terms, it influences what kinds of outputs
the model considers plausible.

514
00:46:24,895 --> 00:46:29,895
The figure shows a process that moves from data toward
noise and a learned process that moves back

515
00:46:29,895 --> 00:46:36,595
toward data. It also includes different mathematical
formulations of those paths. You do not need the
equations to

516
00:46:36,595 --> 00:46:42,935
understand the creative implication. The prior helps
supply details that your inputs leave unspecified.

517
00:46:42,935 --> 00:46:47,095
Those details can be useful, but they are not
guaranteed to be true.

518
00:46:47,095 --> 00:46:53,809
Plausibility should therefore be distinguished from
faithful reconstruction, factual accuracy, and artistic
appropriateness.

519
00:46:54,109 --> 00:46:59,329
The upper diagram shows a denoising U-Net using
information at several spatial scales.

520
00:46:59,329 --> 00:47:03,229
The U shape reflects a process that reduces and then
rebuilds spatial detail,

521
00:47:03,229 --> 00:47:11,849
while connections carry information between stages.
Prompt embeddings provide text conditioning. The time-
step embedding tells the network about

522
00:47:11,849 --> 00:47:17,709
the current noise-removal stage. It does not indicate
the moment of an action inside a video.

523
00:47:17,709 --> 00:47:23,569
As you trace the diagram, identify the noisy input, the
conditions, and the predicted output.

524
00:47:23,569 --> 00:47:29,709
This reading strategy is more important than memorizing
every block. It lets you connect a model's architecture
to

525
00:47:29,709 --> 00:47:32,674
the information available when it makes each
refinement.

526
00:47:32,974 --> 00:47:37,534
The lower diagram shows a common way to extend image-
processing blocks across time.

527
00:47:37,534 --> 00:47:43,554
Spatial operations connect information within each
frame. Temporal operations connect information across
frames.

528
00:47:43,554 --> 00:47:49,374
The legend distinguishes these roles, and the inserted
temporal components show where the sequence gains
additional connections.

529
00:47:49,374 --> 00:47:54,214
This is one architectural family, rather than a
description of every modern video model.

530
00:47:54,214 --> 00:47:58,994
The principle is that treating every frame
independently leaves consistency to chance.

531
00:47:58,994 --> 00:48:04,414
Connecting frames gives the system a way to coordinate
their features. For our moving painting,

532
00:48:04,414 --> 00:48:11,054
such coordination can help preserve structure while
appearance changes. It still needs suitable training
and conditioning to produce

533
00:48:11,054 --> 00:48:13,053
the particular change we want.

534
00:48:13,353 --> 00:48:21,333
Conditioning adds information that guides generation.
ControlNet is a well-known example of a method that
incorporates structural signals

535
00:48:21,333 --> 00:48:27,653
such as edges or depth. The diagram shows a trainable
branch alongside a fixed base network.

536
00:48:27,653 --> 00:48:31,853
At a high level, the added branch learns how to use the
extra condition.

537
00:48:31,853 --> 00:48:35,453
Think of a sketch that places the painting on the left
wall of the studio.

538
00:48:35,453 --> 00:48:40,373
The sketch provides a spatial constraint that a broad
text prompt might not preserve reliably.

539
00:48:40,373 --> 00:48:46,353
Different conditions constrain different properties. An
edge map says little about the intended material or
mood,

540
00:48:46,353 --> 00:48:50,595
so you may still need text, references, and later
selection to complete the result.

541
00:48:50,895 --> 00:48:57,615
Inversion and personalization have different purposes.
Inversion starts with an existing image and seeks a
representation or generation

542
00:48:57,615 --> 00:49:02,995
path that can reproduce it, often approximately. That
representation can then support edits.

543
00:49:02,995 --> 00:49:08,255
Personalization adapts a model or concept
representation to a particular subject or style.

544
00:49:08,255 --> 00:49:13,195
A workflow may use both, but they answer different
questions. If you want to edit one existing studio

545
00:49:13,195 --> 00:49:18,975
image, inversion may be relevant. If you want to
generate many new views of a recurring character,

546
00:49:18,975 --> 00:49:26,615
personalization may be relevant. Neither label
guarantees exact preservation. Check how well the
method reconstructs the input or

547
00:49:26,615 --> 00:49:29,759
maintains the subject under the changes your project
needs.

548
00:49:30,059 --> 00:49:35,879
TokenFlow illustrates video editing that aims to
preserve coherent movement while changing appearance.

549
00:49:35,879 --> 00:49:41,519
The top row shows the input performance. The lower row
changes the subject and object while retaining a

550
00:49:41,519 --> 00:49:48,959
related sequence of poses and positions. The method
uses correspondences between features to help keep the
edit consistent.

551
00:49:48,959 --> 00:49:54,739
This differs from independently generating a new image
for every frame. The example is useful for
understanding the

552
00:49:54,739 --> 00:50:00,299
task, but selected frames cannot show every temporal
issue. For a performance-based art video,

553
00:50:00,299 --> 00:50:06,859
inspect whether the intended gesture remains clear and
whether changing the surface appearance has
unintentionally changed the emotion

554
00:50:06,859 --> 00:50:08,879
or physical meaning of the action.

555
00:50:09,179 --> 00:50:14,859
VideoComposer illustrates a different use of
conditions. Here, an image supplies appearance
information,

556
00:50:14,859 --> 00:50:21,979
while a depth sequence supplies structural guidance
through time. The generated video combines those inputs
with a text

557
00:50:21,979 --> 00:50:28,279
description. Look at how each condition answers a
different question. The image helps specify what the
subject looks

558
00:50:28,279 --> 00:50:35,179
like. Depth helps describe spatial structure and its
progression. The text describes the desired content or
action more

559
00:50:35,179 --> 00:50:41,459
broadly. This is a useful way to plan a controlled
workflow: choose inputs according to the property each

560
00:50:41,459 --> 00:50:50,027
one constrains. More inputs do not automatically
produce a better result, especially when their
instructions or structures conflict.

561
00:50:50,327 --> 00:50:56,167
A three-dimensional object has many views, including
surfaces that a single picture does not show.

562
00:50:56,167 --> 00:51:02,227
The examples display objects under different conditions
and viewpoints. A front view can be attractive while
the side

563
00:51:02,227 --> 00:51:09,427
or back contains implausible geometry. This is a
distinctive requirement of reusable three-dimensional
material.

564
00:51:09,427 --> 00:51:14,067
The representation needs enough consistency to support
the views you intend to render.

565
00:51:14,067 --> 00:51:18,607
For our studio chair, consider whether it will appear
only in the background or whether the camera will

566
00:51:18,607 --> 00:51:25,807
circle it. The second use demands more complete
inspection. Evaluate generated geometry as an object
with a purpose,

567
00:51:25,807 --> 00:51:28,572
rather than as a single favorable preview image.

568
00:51:28,872 --> 00:51:36,092
Shape and appearance are related but distinct outputs.
A mesh describes surfaces through connected geometric
elements.

569
00:51:36,092 --> 00:51:43,072
Materials and textures describe how those surfaces look
when rendered. The figure separates visual examples
from extracted meshes,

570
00:51:43,072 --> 00:51:48,852
making the distinction visible. A plausible texture can
hide a structural problem from one angle.

571
00:51:48,852 --> 00:51:52,812
A clean mesh can also look unfinished until materials
and lighting are added.

572
00:51:52,812 --> 00:51:57,892
If the object must animate, connectivity and
deformation introduce further requirements. For
production,

573
00:51:57,892 --> 00:52:03,372
inspect the representation that you will actually use.
A rendered preview is useful evidence,

574
00:52:03,372 --> 00:52:08,695
but it does not replace checking whether the underlying
asset supports the intended movement and camera views.

575
00:52:08,995 --> 00:52:14,535
A scene adds relationships between objects and spaces.
As a camera moves through a room,

576
00:52:14,535 --> 00:52:22,295
doorways, furniture, and walls should form a coherent
arrangement. The figure illustrates scene-level
generation and related views.

577
00:52:22,295 --> 00:52:27,135
This is more demanding than producing an isolated
object against a simple background.

578
00:52:27,135 --> 00:52:31,135
It is also important to distinguish a video that looks
like a camera move from an editable scene

579
00:52:31,135 --> 00:52:35,315
representation. The video may look convincing without
providing geometry that you can manipulate.

580
00:52:35,315 --> 00:52:41,475
If your project needs to change the path later,
determine whether the workflow actually supports that
operation.

581
00:52:41,475 --> 00:52:46,387
The appearance of spatial movement alone does not
establish control over three-dimensional space.

582
00:52:46,687 --> 00:52:53,627
DreamFusion uses image-based generative guidance to
optimize a three-dimensional result. The basic idea is
to render views of

583
00:52:53,627 --> 00:52:57,447
a candidate scene and use a learned image prior to
guide improvements.

584
00:52:57,447 --> 00:53:02,887
The figure shows outputs from multiple viewpoints,
along with other visualizations of their structure.

585
00:53:02,887 --> 00:53:06,667
This connects the knowledge of an image model to a
different representation.

586
00:53:06,667 --> 00:53:12,647
It is an influential idea because large image
collections can provide information that is difficult
to obtain directly

587
00:53:12,647 --> 00:53:19,987
for every three-dimensional object. However, an image
prior does not automatically ensure agreement across
all views.

588
00:53:19,987 --> 00:53:26,489
Look for repeated features, distorted geometry, or
inconsistent appearances when evaluating a generated
object.

589
00:53:26,789 --> 00:53:34,989
InstructNeRF2NeRF illustrates editing a reconstructed
scene using language instructions. The examples alter
features while showing the results from

590
00:53:34,989 --> 00:53:41,989
related viewpoints. A neural radiance field, or NeRF,
represents a scene through a learned function used for
rendering.

591
00:53:41,989 --> 00:53:48,089
Editing such a representation differs from repainting
one isolated photograph. The goal is for the change to
appear

592
00:53:48,089 --> 00:53:53,149
coherently across views. This can be useful when a
creator wants to revise the appearance of an existing

593
00:53:53,149 --> 00:53:59,649
captured space. It does not imply that every edit
creates physically complete or animation-ready
geometry.

594
00:53:59,649 --> 00:54:06,420
Always connect the demonstration to the operations you
require, such as new camera views or consistent
appearance changes.

595
00:54:06,720 --> 00:54:12,300
The output format changes what we can edit later.
Pixels give us a finished image surface.

596
00:54:12,300 --> 00:54:18,840
A scene representation can support rendering from
additional viewpoints. Movement data can separate an
action from the character

597
00:54:18,840 --> 00:54:25,540
that eventually performs it. Consider two projects: a
fixed-view poster and a camera orbit around a
sculpture.

598
00:54:25,540 --> 00:54:30,000
The poster may need no three-dimensional representation
at all. The orbit does.

599
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:35,600
Choosing a more complex representation can add
flexibility, but also adds preparation and evaluation
work.

600
00:54:35,600 --> 00:54:40,960
Pause and name the most important later edit in your
project. Then ask which output format makes that

601
00:54:40,960 --> 00:54:44,730
edit possible, and what limitations you would need to
test before committing.

602
00:54:45,030 --> 00:54:52,430
Motion generation can produce articulated movement
rather than finished video pixels. The example shows
audio-conditioned movement sequences that

603
00:54:52,430 --> 00:54:56,930
can later be rendered with a character. This separates
what the body does from how the final image

604
00:54:56,930 --> 00:55:02,790
looks. The same movement might be applied to different
character designs, subject to the capabilities of the
rig

605
00:55:02,790 --> 00:55:09,730
and retargeting process. This is valuable when timing
and performance matter more than a particular rendered
appearance.

606
00:55:09,730 --> 00:55:16,230
It also introduces its own evaluation questions. Are
contacts plausible? Does the body maintain balance?

607
00:55:16,230 --> 00:55:21,530
Does the movement relate to the music in a meaningful
way? A visually attractive render can conceal problems

608
00:55:21,530 --> 00:55:23,275
in the underlying motion.

609
00:55:23,575 --> 00:55:30,495
Our final reading is Advances in Artificial
Intelligence: A Review for the Creative Industries by
Anantrasirichai,

610
00:55:30,495 --> 00:55:39,015
Zhang, and Bull, published in Artificial Intelligence
Review in 2026. Its scope extends beyond generation to
analysis,

611
00:55:39,015 --> 00:55:45,815
enhancement, spatial reconstruction, and other parts of
production. Some illustrated examples are older than
the publication date,

612
00:55:45,815 --> 00:55:50,375
so we will keep those dates clear. This wider view
helps explain why improving a generator may not

613
00:55:50,375 --> 00:55:54,575
solve the main problem in a project. The difficulty may
involve selecting a region,

614
00:55:54,575 --> 00:56:00,555
preserving a performance, refining an asset, or judging
a result. We now connect those supporting capabilities
to the

615
00:56:00,555 --> 00:56:02,139
creative workflow as a whole.

616
00:56:02,439 --> 00:56:09,419
Creation sits inside a larger process. Before
generation, we establish an intention and gather
material that helps communicate

617
00:56:09,419 --> 00:56:17,079
it. After generation, we select, revise, combine, and
review the results. Sound and editing can change the
experience

618
00:56:17,079 --> 00:56:23,119
substantially. Think about where time actually goes in
your own work. If the problem is unclear pacing,

619
00:56:23,119 --> 00:56:26,939
generating a sharper image may not help. If the problem
is identity drift,

620
00:56:26,939 --> 00:56:32,839
a new music track may not help. Identify the stage
responsible for the mismatch between intention and
result.

621
00:56:32,839 --> 00:56:36,679
This lets you choose a targeted intervention and
evaluate whether it resolves the problem,

622
00:56:36,679 --> 00:56:40,129
instead of treating every issue as a reason to generate
again.

623
00:56:40,429 --> 00:56:45,929
This figure compares image examples produced from a
prompt and includes an editing comparison.

624
00:56:45,929 --> 00:56:53,709
The examples are dated November 27, 2024. They are
historical illustrations, not a current product
ranking.

625
00:56:53,709 --> 00:56:59,729
Look at composition, object relationships, and the
interpretation of style. A shared prompt can leave room
for noticeably

626
00:56:59,729 --> 00:57:05,489
different results because models and generation
settings differ. Even one model can vary across
samples.

627
00:57:05,489 --> 00:57:09,589
For a fair creative comparison, decide which visible
properties matter and inspect more than one output.

628
00:57:09,589 --> 00:57:15,209
Declaring a winner from a single attractive picture
tells us little about reliability or whether the system
can

629
00:57:15,209 --> 00:57:17,906
produce the specific sequence our project requires.

630
00:57:18,206 --> 00:57:22,446
The enlarged editing example asks for a white car to
become red.

631
00:57:22,446 --> 00:57:27,406
This sounds local, but we should inspect the whole
image. Did the second car remain stable?

632
00:57:27,406 --> 00:57:33,026
Did the road, lighting, and viewpoint change? A method
can satisfy the most obvious part of the request

633
00:57:33,026 --> 00:57:40,026
while introducing unwanted differences elsewhere. The
figure presents selected historical examples, so it
should not be used as

634
00:57:40,026 --> 00:57:46,666
a current benchmark. Its teaching value is the
evaluation procedure. Define the intended change and
the features to

635
00:57:46,666 --> 00:57:52,566
preserve, then inspect both. This is the same procedure
we would use when editing the painting inside our

636
00:57:52,566 --> 00:57:54,552
otherwise stable studio scene.

637
00:57:54,852 --> 00:58:00,792
Performance can come from different kinds of source
information. The left example involves captured body
movement and its

638
00:58:00,792 --> 00:58:06,692
use with an animated figure. The right example combines
an image with audio for facial animation.

639
00:58:06,692 --> 00:58:12,992
These are separate systems, and their inputs constrain
different properties. A body-motion reference tells us
something about pose

640
00:58:12,992 --> 00:58:20,032
and timing. Audio can guide speech-related facial
movement. A portrait supplies appearance but does not
itself specify a

641
00:58:20,032 --> 00:58:24,532
whole performance. When planning a character shot,
identify what you already have:

642
00:58:24,532 --> 00:58:30,552
a voice recording, an acted movement, or only a design.
The available material helps determine which workflow
is

643
00:58:30,552 --> 00:58:32,905
appropriate and what the model must invent.

644
00:58:33,205 --> 00:58:41,345
Post-production continues after generation. The figure
places image enhancement beside portrait editing to
show different kinds of intervention.

645
00:58:41,345 --> 00:58:48,125
Enhancement may improve apparent detail or resolution.
Editing changes selected content or expression.

646
00:58:48,125 --> 00:58:53,105
Both can be useful, but they require evaluation
according to the intended task.

647
00:58:53,105 --> 00:58:58,565
A sharper face is not necessarily a more faithful face,
and an expressive alteration may affect identity.

648
00:58:58,565 --> 00:59:03,085
For an art video, you might welcome some changes while
rejecting others.

649
00:59:03,085 --> 00:59:07,425
Keep an original version so that you can compare what
happened. It is easier to judge a revision

650
00:59:07,425 --> 00:59:12,516
when the intended improvement is specific and the
earlier state remains available for reference.

651
00:59:12,816 --> 00:59:20,136
Compare the low-resolution input, the AI-upscaled
image, and the high-resolution reference. Pay attention
to texture rather than just

652
00:59:20,136 --> 00:59:26,076
overall sharpness. The upscaled version can create
convincing detail that differs from the reference.

653
00:59:26,076 --> 00:59:31,556
This happens because a model uses learned regularities
to fill in information that the input does not fully

654
00:59:31,556 --> 00:59:36,816
contain. In a new artwork, that contribution may be
acceptable or desirable.

655
00:59:36,816 --> 00:59:42,536
In faithful restoration, it may be a problem. For
video, repeat the comparison across time to check
whether

656
00:59:42,536 --> 00:59:47,156
the added texture flickers. The evaluation should
follow the purpose of the work,

657
00:59:47,156 --> 00:59:51,189
rather than assuming that more visible detail always
means better quality.

658
00:59:51,489 --> 00:59:57,929
Segmentation identifies regions or objects in an image.
Tracking follows an object through a sequence.

659
00:59:57,929 --> 01:00:03,589
Together, they can make local video editing more
manageable. Suppose we want to alter the returning
person's coat

660
01:00:03,589 --> 01:00:09,109
while leaving the room unchanged. We need to identify
the coat and keep the selected region attached to

661
01:00:09,109 --> 01:00:15,929
it as the person moves. The figure organizes several
object-understanding tasks and includes segmentation
examples.

662
01:00:15,929 --> 01:00:21,749
These capabilities support generation and editing
without necessarily generating the final content
themselves.

663
01:00:21,749 --> 01:00:26,809
They are part of the larger workflow. Failures around
occlusion or fast movement can cause an edit to

664
01:00:26,809 --> 01:00:29,989
jump, leak into the background, or disappear
temporarily.

665
01:00:30,289 --> 01:00:34,909
Fine boundaries are difficult because small selection
errors can be highly visible.

666
01:00:34,909 --> 01:00:38,529
Look at the butterfly outline and the thin structure in
the comparison.

667
01:00:38,529 --> 01:00:43,809
Hair, transparent material, and narrow gaps create
similar challenges in creative work.

668
01:00:43,809 --> 01:00:50,929
The figure compares selected outputs from SAM and HQ-
SAM. It illustrates the importance of boundary detail,

669
01:00:50,929 --> 01:00:55,929
rather than proving that one method always performs
better. A mask that looks acceptable in a still frame

670
01:00:55,929 --> 01:01:00,969
may still change inconsistently during movement. For
our studio, the edge of the painting is a useful
inspection

671
01:01:00,969 --> 01:01:07,109
point. A clean interior transformation can be
undermined by a shifting outline or an edit that spills
onto

672
01:01:07,109 --> 01:01:07,787
the frame.

673
01:01:08,087 --> 01:01:12,907
NeRFs and Gaussian splats are different ways to
represent scenes for rendering.

674
01:01:12,907 --> 01:01:18,047
A NeRF uses a learned field that describes properties
queried along viewing rays.

675
01:01:18,047 --> 01:01:23,267
Gaussian splatting uses many spatial primitives with
properties such as position and appearance.

676
01:01:23,267 --> 01:01:29,027
The figure places their processes alongside examples of
spatial interaction. You do not need to master their
mathematics

677
01:01:29,027 --> 01:01:33,347
to understand the production question: what views can
this representation render well,

678
01:01:33,347 --> 01:01:37,287
and what can I edit? Capture coverage and
reconstruction quality still matter.

679
01:01:37,287 --> 01:01:44,607
A scene representation does not automatically contain
reliable hidden surfaces. Test the intended viewpoint
range before building a

680
01:01:44,607 --> 01:01:47,548
film or interactive experience around that freedom.

681
01:01:47,848 --> 01:01:53,548
Quality has several meanings. An image can be sharp but
fail to convey the intended action.

682
01:01:53,548 --> 01:01:59,428
A sequence can match a prompt yet feel emotionally
wrong. A distorted image can also serve a deliberate

683
01:01:59,428 --> 01:02:06,568
artistic purpose. These observations call for separate
judgments, rather than one vague claim that the output
is good.

684
01:02:06,568 --> 01:02:09,948
For the studio, you could assess technical continuity,
the clarity of the reveal,

685
01:02:09,948 --> 01:02:14,268
and the audience's interpretation. Different viewers
may disagree about the last part,

686
01:02:14,268 --> 01:02:20,168
and that disagreement can be useful evidence. Explain
the criteria you chose and why they suit the work.

687
01:02:20,168 --> 01:02:25,261
Automated measures can contribute, but they cannot
settle every question about artistic experience.

688
01:02:25,561 --> 01:02:30,241
A process record helps other people understand what you
made and how you made it.

689
01:02:30,241 --> 01:02:37,101
Save important references and meaningful before-and-
after versions. Record model names and versions when
available,

690
01:02:37,101 --> 01:02:42,741
along with settings needed to repeat a comparison.
Explain the decisions that changed the work,

691
01:02:42,741 --> 01:02:49,361
including choices made in editing or sound. This is
more informative than presenting a final output with
only

692
01:02:49,361 --> 01:02:54,541
a prompt. It also helps you diagnose your own workflow
when a later result differs.

693
01:02:54,541 --> 01:03:01,341
For coursework, follow the assignment's actual
requirements. The broader habit is to make your
contribution and source material

694
01:03:01,341 --> 01:03:07,075
understandable without turning the record into an
unstructured collection of every experiment.

695
01:03:07,375 --> 01:03:12,055
We have used five readings to follow a creative process
from model behavior to finished work.

696
01:03:12,055 --> 01:03:16,075
The video survey explains how sequences form and how
inputs constrain them.

697
01:03:16,075 --> 01:03:22,715
The visual-art survey connects methods to intention.
The film survey shows creators combining tools and
making production decisions.

698
01:03:22,715 --> 01:03:31,295
Visual computing explains how related ideas transfer
across different representations. The creative-
industries review places generation alongside
supporting tools

699
01:03:31,295 --> 01:03:35,975
and evaluation. Choose one decision you would now
change in your own project.

700
01:03:35,975 --> 01:03:41,655
Explain which reading informed it, what visible result
you expect, and how you would test that expectation.

701
01:03:41,655 --> 01:03:47,071
That connection between a method, an intention, and
evidence is the central skill to take from today's
lecture.
